body:
Thanks for the report!

Looking at the code in the MRP:

```
func _ready() -> void:
	location_manager = JavaClassWrapper.wrap("android.location.LocationManager")
	var location_functions = location_manager.get_java_method_list()
	if location_manager:
		for entry in location_functions:
			if entry.name == "hasProvider":
				var has_it : bool = location_manager.hasProvider("gps")
				print(has_it)
```

What this code is doing is trying to call the `hasProvider()` method directly on the `LocationManager` class, but `hasProvider()` isn't a static method, it needs to be called on an instance of the class. In order to get the `LocationManager` instance, you need to get it from the application context.

This code should work:

```
func _ready() -> void:
	var android_runtime = Engine.get_singleton("AndroidRuntime")
	if android_runtime:
		var location_manager = android_runtime.getApplicationContext().getSystemService("location")
		var has_it : bool = location_manager.hasProvider("gps")
		print(has_it)
```

So, I think the problem here is mainly that the error message is confusing. It's currently:

```
Attempt to call function 'hasProvider' in base 'JavaClass' on a null instance.
```

That message comes from the GDScript VM, in response to `JavaClass` returning `CALL_ERROR_INSTANCE_IS_NULL` when trying to call a non-static method on a class.

We could try and improve that?

> How do I expect it to work:
> Proper Error Handling and / or not crashing the whole app :D

I'm not sure I'd count this as a "crash". If you run the app from the editor via the "Remote Debug" button, you end up with the debugger coming up in the editor, with the (admittedly not very good) error message.

If you run a release build, the execution of the GDScript will stop when this error is encountered, but the app it won't fully crash. Other scripts will be unaffected, or if a function in the same script is executed again later, it will run. This is the same behavior if you call a non-existent method on any object in GDScript.

So, I think we just need to make the error message better when calling a non-static method on a class, rather than an instance.
